  "body": "This is mostly to get @allenwb's opinion.\n\nWe are writing our algorithms ES-spec style, for precision. However, since this isn't actually the ES spec, we have the opportunity to depart a bit in favor of brevity when we can do so without causing ambiguity.\n\nTwo of the most annoying things in writing ES-spec text are:\n- Having to alias **this**, e.g. \"Let _O_ be the **this** value\"\n- Having to say long-winded phrases like \"Let _length_ be the value of _O_’s [[ArrayBufferByteLength]] internal slot.\"\n\nI'd like to avoid both of these things. Currently our text is not very consistent but does stuff like `this.[[ArrayBufferLength]]` or similar, which is not congruous with the ES spec where .[[x]] is only used for record fields and for some reason nobody ever refers to **this** directly.\n\nMy proposal is **this**→[[ArrayBufferLength]], to fix both of those problems. Since EcmaSpeak doesn't have closures, **this** is always unambiguous, so it shouldn't be necessary to alias it. And the → instead of . differentiates \"internal slot\" from \"record field.\"\n",
